    "created_at": "2017-02-08T14:18:17Z",
    "updated_at": "2017-02-08T14:18:17Z",
    "body": "The stuff around decode that already exist in the spec is:\r\n\r\nhttps://html.spec.whatwg.org/#img-good\r\nhttps://html.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-imagebitmap-good\r\n\r\n\\#img-good is only defined for the current request (\"The current request is usually referred to as the img element itself.\"). The pending request is basically hidden right now. But I need to think more about the implications for `decode()` here... some cases cause the current request to be replaced, while some cause it to be mutated. It's not super-clear then what it means for \"current request is changed\".\r\n\r\nAnyway, \"fully decodable\" is then used by https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#check-the-usability-of-the-image-argument and that in turn is used by [`createPattern()`](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#dom-context-2d-createpattern) and [`drawImage()`](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#dom-context-2d-drawimage). I think these methods implicitly do a synchronous decode if the image is fully decodable but not currently decoded in memory. Maybe we should define the concept to \"decode\" somewhere and invoke that here and in `decode()`? Although the `decode()` API also needs to wait for network...\r\n\r\nFrom what I understand from this PR this is supposed to work:\r\n\r\n```\r\nvar img = new Image();\r\nimg.decode().then(...);\r\nimg.src = \"foo\";\r\n```\r\n\r\ncorrect?\r\n\r\nFor `picture`/`srcset`, when https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#reacting-to-environment-changes runs and the new image is loaded, calls https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#upgrade-the-pending-request-to-the-current-request which replaces the current request. So that can reject `decode()` if it happens before the previous image is completely decoded. Is that what we want?",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
