    "created_at": "2017-03-24T07:38:43Z",
    "updated_at": "2017-03-24T07:38:43Z",
    "body": "> Another item I ran into while playing around with the implementation are partially loaded images. The way I'm reasoning about this is that when a decode() is called, the promise resolves only when the image is fully loaded and decoded. That is, I don't think there is any benefit for us to decode whatever we currently have loaded and resolve the callback, since appending that item to the dom might still be slow if more bytes have loaded since the promise resolved. I think we should mention something about this in the spec. \r\n\r\nHmm. Correct me if I am wrong, but I believe this is taken care of in the spec. In particular, it will wait until the image becomes completely available, then will decode, then will fulfill the promise.\r\n\r\n> Specifically, what I have now is that the decode()'s promise resolution implies that load event has happened (in fact my prototype waits for load if the image isn't complete yet).\r\n\r\nYes, that is how it is specced, I am pretty sure!\r\n\r\n> However, this becomes a little awkward for animated GIFs (or other animated images), where the load, AFAIK, is fired when the full GIF is loaded, but the decode is only going to decode the first frame. I think that might still be a reasonable behavior, since subsequent frames of the GIF are at least available, if not decoded, at the time the promise resolves. This might be something that we want to discuss/spec though.\r\n\r\nInteresting point. I'll include a note in the spec.",
